# A committed TEMPLATE of .github/c2-serving-job.env for the tp4/upload-1 window (docs/tp4-upload1.md; scripts/ci/c2_serving_job.py parses it): copy it over
# .github/c2-serving-job.env on a throwaway commit of the branch and push a tag experiment/c2-serving-vN, ONE TAG AT A TIME (wait for the run to finish; never cancel a running card job).
# Nothing here names a rig, a card, a host or a registry: the cards are resolved at run time. The jobs are templates only: no tag is pushed from the branch by its author. ORDER.txt has the
# order, the stop rules, the minutes and the read rules.
# ONE IMAGE FOR THE WHOLE WINDOW: C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-upload-1-1 is a PLACEHOLDER for the tag B0 builds from the pushed head of tp4/upload-1 (the ship-5 stack plus the host tier's upload levers, qwen_tier_upload;
# substitute it in every template with one command: sed -i 's/tp4-upload-1-1/<tag>/' *.env). THE TAG MUST BE FRESH: the build step skips a tag that exists. The levers live in files the image carries
# (qwen_tier_upload.py, qwen_prefix_registry.py, qwen_prefix_scheduler_patch.py): an image built before them ignores the three names and the arm reads NOT_EXERCISED (no "host tier upload on" line).
# The cards are under development: this window never stops, starts or hands back the node agent (production is down by the owner's choice while it runs).
# U1 (STOP): the AUDITED exactness arm of the upload levers (c2_prefix_gate.py --plan tier-upload, scenario tier_upload = the tier-timed cycle at ~64k, ~128k and ~254k) on the ship-5 audited twin
# c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-fx-s5-audit with QWEN_PREFIX_TIER_IO_THREADS=4 and QWEN_PREFIX_TIER_VERIFY_THREADS=4 added by the gate (no profile twin: the profile already audits every restored block, verify=all, the
# preconverted comparison, the engine-start zero fill's byte audit and the extent audit). Per size: the cold prefill, the device-resident hit, fresh-salt floods the size of the KV pool so the session leaves the
# device pool for host RAM, the returning hit (restored from host RAM through the threaded IO, every block read back and compared with the bytes written, every digest checked) and its COLD TWIN (a fresh
# salt, the same body, compared whole). THE QUESTION: is a restore done by threaded, pipelined staging copies byte-identical to a cold prefill at 64k, 128k and 254k? READ: the plan verdict PASS; the
# three cold/hit pairs IDENTICAL; the line "host tier upload on io_threads=4 io_chunk_blocks=0 verify_threads=4 io_wrapped=1" and NO "host tier upload fell back"; the three "host tier restore" lines
# (blocks, GB, ms) and the arm's lines "threaded restore writes: N blocks in W ms wall; D ms inside device calls, S ms of staging work" (W below D plus S is the overlap), "threaded spill reads" and "parallel
# digest checks"; zero tier_audit_mismatches, tier_digest_failures, tier_restore_failures, tier_latched; the program count unchanged; c2_smoke_check clean (its fusion table holds the three lever rows).
# ESTIMATE (nothing of this has run on a card): the audited restore of 254k is slow by design (every block hashed at 1.5 to 2.5 GB/s and read back), about 10 s; the arm's cost is the floods and
# the cold twins, about 100 minutes. NO-GO for the levers: any pair that is not IDENTICAL, any audit mismatch, a restore that did not go through the threaded IO (tier_io_write_blocks 0).
# Unqualified: a measurement, never evidence. Estimate: 100 minutes.
C2_CARDS=quad
C2_ACTIONS=reset prefix
C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-upload-1-1
C2_PREFIX_PLAN=tier-upload
C2_PREFIX_PROFILE=c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-fx-s5-audit
C2_PREFIX_BASELINE=none
C2_MODEL_REPO=ukisai/Swift-1.5-Qwen3.8-27b
C2_MODEL_REVISION=b4c84d42903a8646b25857eb2827288d92ed85a4
C2_BOX_MINUTES=180
